== Advanced Combat

Wave 0 taught you the loop. This chapter teaches you to *own* it.

=== Switch Skills & Scroll Swap

Every weapon has alternate moves — *Switch Skills* — unlocked through quests and requests. In Master Rank you get the real prize: *Scroll Swap*, which lets you carry two complete loadouts (Scrolls Red and Blue) and switch between them mid-hunt.

The power is not "two movesets" — it is *role switching*:

- One scroll for aggression (damage silkbinds, offensive switch skills), one for survival (counters, utility).
- Swap when the monster's state changes — enraged means you want the defensive scroll.
- Some skills trigger effects on swap; learn your weapon's swap bonuses.

#tip[
  Build both scrolls around the *same weapon* but *different fights*: the monster's enrage state is your cue to swap, not a reminder to panic.
]

=== Counters: The Mastery Layer

Every weapon has at least one defensive move that turns an incoming hit into an offense — *counters, guards, parries*:

- Long Sword: foresight-style parries that refill your gauge.
- Lance: guard counters that answer every poke with one of your own.
- Great Sword: shoulder tackle trades your hitbox for super armor.
- Charge Blade: guard-point timing turns blocks into explosive punishes.

The pattern is universal: *read the wind-up, trigger the counter, punish the recovery.* This is the skill ceiling of the game, and it is learnable — one monster at a time.

=== Element, Status, Affinity — the Real Damage Math

- *Element* (fire/water/thunder/ice/dragon) adds flat damage per hit on monsters weak to it. Fast weapons (Dual Blades, Sword & Shield, Bow) love element; slow ones prefer raw.
- *Status* (poison/paralysis/sleep/blast) builds a hidden meter; when it procs, the monster suffers the effect. Status weapons are support tools and crowd-control: paralysis is a free beating, sleep is a free bomb wake-up.
- *Affinity* is crit chance; critical hits deal more damage. Skills like *Weakness Exploit* make affinity the highest-value stat in the game — because it rewards exactly the behavior you should already be doing (hitting weak spots).

#note[
  The in-game Hunter's Notes list every monster's element/status weaknesses per part. Treat it as the reference — this guide deliberately stays out of the numbers game, because the matchup changes every hunt.
]

=== Part Breaking as a Strategy

Wave 0 said "break parts for rewards." Now break parts *for fight control*:

- *Severable tails* — cut them and the monster loses its tail attacks (and you gain carve materials).
- *Breakable heads* — many monsters' nastiest attacks come from the head; breaking it weakens or disables them.
- *Flinch thresholds* — parts have damage thresholds; hitting them flinches the monster and resets its rhythm. Chain part breaks to keep pressure windows open.

Plan your targeting *before* the hunt: what part do you break first, and with what tool (sever via cutting damage, break via blunt/impact)?

=== Wyvern Riding as a Weapon

Riding is not a novelty — it is tactical control:

- Ride the *other* monster into your target for massive damage and a knockdown.
- Use the wall slam for the guaranteed down; use the puny aerial finisher when you need positioning instead.
- In multiplayer, a ride is the team's biggest burst window — call it out (or let the auto-call do it) so the team stops and unloads.

=== Reading Monsters: The Learning Loop

1. *Watch* a monster's moveset in its enrage state first — that is the dangerous version you must survive.
2. *Map* each attack to a response: dodge-through, block, counter, or punish.
3. *Exploit* the recovery: the move that almost hits you is the opening you were waiting for.

This loop — watch, map, exploit — is the entire endgame skill. Monsters only ever add new animations; they never add new rules.
